1
00:00:02,423 --> 00:00:15,473
[SPEAKER_00]: I was reflecting today on what is the common thread... what is in common to all... everything that's unwholesome.

2
00:00:15,773 --> 00:00:21,218
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... what is the common thread between everything that's unwholesome?

3
00:00:21,278 --> 00:00:21,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Unwholesome acts.

4
00:00:24,340 --> 00:00:28,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I've got my own thoughts on that, but... what do you think?

5
00:00:29,324 --> 00:00:30,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, tell me what you reflected.

6
00:00:36,352 --> 00:00:48,843
[SPEAKER_00]: To me, it's anything, when you attempt to take away that which is, would conceal that which is with that which isn't.

7
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:58,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh... certain contradiction then, there's a certain discrepancy.

8
00:00:58,832 --> 00:00:59,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:00:59,332 --> 00:01:01,074
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's in common to everything unwholesome.

10
00:01:01,334 --> 00:01:01,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

11
00:01:02,573 --> 00:01:10,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if something is unwholesome, means there is a... it contains a discrepancy in itself, which is what contradiction is.

12
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:15,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that goes against the way things are.

13
00:01:15,223 --> 00:01:18,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this, and I try to remove that and replace it with something else.

14
00:01:20,728 --> 00:01:26,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's it's not just like go against the way things are, but in itself is against things are.

15
00:01:26,753 --> 00:01:29,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that contradiction is in unwholesome.

16
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:33,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not between wholesome and unwholesome.

17
00:01:34,011 --> 00:01:37,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying unwholesome things are unwholesome because they go against wholesome.

18
00:01:39,299 --> 00:01:43,244
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they are unwholesome because in themselves they have contradiction.

19
00:01:46,008 --> 00:01:46,329
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

20
00:01:49,377 --> 00:01:50,799
[SPEAKER_00]: If I think about the precepts.

21
00:01:52,081 --> 00:01:56,727
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can look at each precept and you can see that same thread running through it.

22
00:01:58,190 --> 00:01:59,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Take for killing, for example.

23
00:02:00,172 --> 00:02:04,359
[SPEAKER_00]: I am taking away that which is not mine.

24
00:02:04,379 --> 00:02:04,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

25
00:02:05,489 --> 00:02:08,090
[SPEAKER_00]: I have no right, I have no...

26
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:23,315
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not entitled to your life or to any other being's life, not even to my own life, but to take something which is not mine and take it away, take it as though it were mine and destroy it.

27
00:02:23,335 --> 00:02:23,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

28
00:02:24,555 --> 00:02:27,716
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... Yeah, so it's a contradiction.

29
00:02:27,756 --> 00:02:27,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

30
00:02:28,477 --> 00:02:29,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that's not yours,

31
00:02:34,058 --> 00:02:38,042
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically destroying, as if it were yours, as if you're in charge, as if you're in control.

32
00:02:39,163 --> 00:02:40,444
[SPEAKER_00]: This is mind to use, is it?

33
00:02:40,805 --> 00:02:49,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Also, another thing is... Life is implicitly valuable for everyone.

34
00:02:51,295 --> 00:02:52,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Even, by the way, people who decide to...

35
00:02:57,117 --> 00:03:04,761
[SPEAKER_01]: People who don't necessarily have a view that the life is worth living, still they can only do that on the basis of being alive.

36
00:03:07,042 --> 00:03:09,623
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why everybody will fear death, except Arahant.

37
00:03:10,143 --> 00:03:10,784
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

38
00:03:10,904 --> 00:03:14,125
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't be ready for death, even if you want to kill yourself.

39
00:03:15,126 --> 00:03:17,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It will surprise you, what death is.

40
00:03:17,707 --> 00:03:20,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Only Arahant is truly unmoved in the face of death, as the Buddha would say.

41
00:03:21,809 --> 00:03:23,070
[SPEAKER_01]: That means that life in itself

42
00:03:24,332 --> 00:03:28,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Being alive is taken at its face value and it has that value.

43
00:03:28,656 --> 00:03:34,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you, as the one who implicitly values life, it's taking life of another.

44
00:03:35,363 --> 00:03:39,367
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another discrepancy of the values, pretty much.

45
00:03:40,328 --> 00:03:45,173
[SPEAKER_01]: While you value life, you're now acting towards destroying life, intentionally.

46
00:03:47,037 --> 00:03:49,878
[SPEAKER_00]: As though life were not valuable.

47
00:03:50,038 --> 00:03:50,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

48
00:03:50,798 --> 00:03:51,779
[SPEAKER_00]: As though life is not valuable.

49
00:03:52,179 --> 00:03:55,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that to me, it's not like a contradiction between wholesome and unwholesome.

50
00:03:56,101 --> 00:04:00,983
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's within yourself, within your own values, within your own principles.

51
00:04:01,603 --> 00:04:08,106
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually, that's why unwholesome is universally unwholesome for everyone, because for everyone it contradicts those universal values.

52
00:04:08,566 --> 00:04:10,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is valuable within you.

53
00:04:11,521 --> 00:04:15,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you say my life is not valuable, you can only say that on the basis of life which you value.

54
00:04:15,262 --> 00:04:16,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're trying to cover it up.

55
00:04:16,703 --> 00:04:18,823
[SPEAKER_00]: You're trying to cover up and conceal that value.

56
00:04:20,084 --> 00:04:21,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Not explicitly, no.

57
00:04:21,084 --> 00:04:21,964
[SPEAKER_01]: That's implied in it.

58
00:04:22,104 --> 00:04:24,445
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not even completely think about it at all, whatsoever.

59
00:04:24,465 --> 00:04:24,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

60
00:04:24,925 --> 00:04:26,466
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean... You're not trying to cover it up.

61
00:04:26,486 --> 00:04:27,246
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just...

62
00:04:28,086 --> 00:04:31,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging in unwholesome means that's the discrepancy you're accepting.

63
00:04:31,607 --> 00:04:32,047
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, yeah.

64
00:04:32,688 --> 00:04:36,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you have to do is not think about unwholesome as... All you need to do is not understand unwh

65
00:04:41,817 --> 00:04:46,701
[SPEAKER_00]: You're born up, you're born into that kind of concealment already.

66
00:04:47,842 --> 00:04:48,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much, yes, pretty much.

67
00:04:48,862 --> 00:04:58,129
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like you start now... You're not like born with the fully developed concealments, but as the Buddha said, all the underlying tendencies for those concealments are there present in the newborn.

68
00:04:58,289 --> 00:04:59,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they will grow, they will develop.

69
00:05:00,271 --> 00:05:04,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Your... that ignorance there, it's not something that you decided to do before.

70
00:05:04,534 --> 00:05:04,714
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

71
00:05:04,974 --> 00:05:06,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you made a choice when you were five years old.

72
00:05:06,776 --> 00:05:07,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

73
00:05:07,356 --> 00:05:08,397
[SPEAKER_00]: I will conceal things now.

74
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:08,857
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

75
00:05:08,877 --> 00:05:09,658
[SPEAKER_00]: The truth of things.

76
00:05:10,318 --> 00:05:10,959
[SPEAKER_00]: This is all mine.

77
00:05:11,857 --> 00:05:12,858
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically ignorance.

78
00:05:12,938 --> 00:05:18,946
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of pretty much the way you attend to things.

79
00:05:18,966 --> 00:05:19,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's implicit in it.

80
00:05:21,048 --> 00:05:24,592
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, when you attend to something, you automatically have to ignore something else.

81
00:05:24,913 --> 00:05:26,054
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot attend to everything.

82
00:05:26,134 --> 00:05:26,915
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's that's all.

83
00:05:27,055 --> 00:05:28,677
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the principle of avijjā, by the way.

84
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:33,793
[SPEAKER_01]: By looking this way, you will not be able to look at the other way at the same time.

85
00:05:34,614 --> 00:05:37,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is the most common way that people choose to look?

86
00:05:38,336 --> 00:05:39,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the way of least resistance.

87
00:05:40,518 --> 00:05:41,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the way of sensuality.

88
00:05:42,159 --> 00:05:43,180
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the way of ill-will.

89
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:45,522
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the way of passion, desire, satisfaction.

90
00:05:45,882 --> 00:05:49,525
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the way, basically, of the grain, with the flow.

91
00:05:51,026 --> 00:05:55,209
[SPEAKER_01]: So, automatically, simply attending to that way means you're not attending to the other way.

92
00:05:55,429 --> 00:05:57,411
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot attend to the both at the same time.

93
00:05:58,398 --> 00:05:59,219
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to learn.

94
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:12,634
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to find a good reason to start looking upstream and then start swimming upstream, while everything is trying to pull you down downstream.

95
00:06:13,915 --> 00:06:15,417
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just in the precepts again.

96
00:06:16,383 --> 00:06:28,291
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, and by the way, I just want to say, that's why... that's why the only one who truly understands wholesome as wholesome and unwholesome as unwholesome, as in the universal value of wholesome and unwholesome, is a sotāpanna.

97
00:06:29,292 --> 00:06:33,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know what's truly skillful and what's truly unskillful, unless you have the right view.

98
00:06:34,396 --> 00:06:41,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Or that whole picture... Well, that's what the right view is, understanding the universal value of wholesome and unwholesome, and then you know.

99
00:06:43,130 --> 00:06:45,972
[SPEAKER_01]: You will know the root of wholesome and the root of unwholesome.

100
00:06:46,893 --> 00:06:48,935
[SPEAKER_01]: And when they asked the Buddha, so what is Sotāpanna?

101
00:06:48,955 --> 00:06:59,022
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, Sotāpanna is the one who knows skillful as skillful, or kusala as kusala, wholesome as wholesome, and he knows unwholesome as unwholesome, akusala as akusala.

102
00:06:59,082 --> 00:07:08,850
[SPEAKER_00]: So, can you say that, then, that which is unwholesome is that which goes against the way things are?

103
00:07:11,693 --> 00:07:16,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, but I don't like using the way things are, because that had been overused and quite mystified already.

104
00:07:16,995 --> 00:07:17,956
[SPEAKER_00]: Goes against the truth.

105
00:07:18,056 --> 00:07:20,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It goes against the universal principles.

106
00:07:20,817 --> 00:07:21,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

107
00:07:21,337 --> 00:07:22,577
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the universal principles?

108
00:07:22,597 --> 00:07:23,518
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the dhamma is.

109
00:07:23,658 --> 00:07:26,239
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is the universal law, as the Buddha would say.

110
00:07:26,719 --> 00:07:27,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Goes against the nature.

111
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:28,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Goes against the dhamma.

112
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:29,600
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

113
00:07:30,461 --> 00:07:32,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So, any... that's...

114
00:07:32,501 --> 00:07:33,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to understand the dhamma.

115
00:07:35,612 --> 00:07:38,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, be a sotāpanna, to know what those universal principles are.

116
00:07:39,213 --> 00:07:49,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can't have an idea of what the natural and nature's law is, and then define, infer from there what is wholesome.

117
00:07:49,935 --> 00:07:52,955
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... that can only be on the level of that kind of abstract view.

118
00:07:53,856 --> 00:07:56,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It can correspond to the practical application to some extent.

119
00:07:56,576 --> 00:08:03,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people can kind of have an accurate... but you haven't truly understood the nature of wholesome as wholesome.

120
00:08:03,638 --> 00:08:05,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why is wholesome wholesome?

121
00:08:05,958 --> 00:08:07,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see it for yourself?

122
00:08:08,199 --> 00:08:09,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is unwholesome unwholesome?

123
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:11,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see that for yourself?

124
00:08:14,162 --> 00:08:16,443
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if we just go to the next, that precept of...

125
00:08:16,944 --> 00:08:19,965
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's not stealing.

126
00:08:21,926 --> 00:08:25,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Not taking something which is not given to you.

127
00:08:25,828 --> 00:08:29,130
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's the whole... you're taking away something which isn't yours.

128
00:08:29,150 --> 00:08:33,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can also... that's why, like, you cannot accidentally break the precepts.

129
00:08:34,674 --> 00:08:38,455
[SPEAKER_01]: The perception is the fundamental factor.

130
00:08:38,535 --> 00:08:48,677
[SPEAKER_01]: If you perceive something as truly yours, and you take it because you perceive it as yours, you mistaken it for something else that looks the same that was yours, there's no stealing involved there.

131
00:08:49,358 --> 00:08:56,879
[SPEAKER_01]: Stealing requires your intentional effort to override, which is what the discrepancy is.

132
00:08:56,899 --> 00:08:59,200
[SPEAKER_01]: So, intentional effort to override the value of life.

133
00:09:00,336 --> 00:09:02,137
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is implicit, even if you don't think about it.

134
00:09:02,717 --> 00:09:08,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentional effort to override the value of ownership, which is implicit, but you choose to ignore it.

135
00:09:08,638 --> 00:09:21,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentional effort to go and, I don't know, if it's the five precepts to seduce other people's partners, or cheat... Again, all of the discrepancies that you then intentionally choose to ignore, and double down in your action towards it.

136
00:09:21,662 --> 00:09:23,302
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why these things are unwholesome.

137
00:09:25,600 --> 00:09:35,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have no perception, like in the sutta, as we mentioned it before, like accidentally, there was an accident and somebody died as a result of it.

138
00:09:35,846 --> 00:09:37,187
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't an offense of killing.

139
00:09:38,108 --> 00:09:47,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Like when there's that story in the Vinaya of the two monks who got in an argument, and then only one of them had a water filter, and he didn't want to give it to the other one.

140
00:09:48,722 --> 00:09:58,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were, like, I'm not giving his mind, he offended him or something, and then the other monk died of thirst, because he didn't want to drink it to break the rule, so he died of thirst.

141
00:09:59,014 --> 00:10:01,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Passed out and couldn't recover and then died.

142
00:10:02,479 --> 00:10:06,984
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first monk wasn't parājika, it wasn't an offense of killing another monk.

143
00:10:07,485 --> 00:10:07,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

144
00:10:08,476 --> 00:10:13,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it would have said, ok, well, next time you should not withhold anything out of ill will.

145
00:10:13,218 --> 00:10:16,318
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it was an offense of a completely different kind.

146
00:10:16,999 --> 00:10:19,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Not on the level of, oh, he's not a monk anymore, because he killed another monk.

147
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:19,880
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

148
00:10:20,040 --> 00:10:20,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to intend.

149
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:26,342
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that other monk said, I will not give you the water, with the intention of, he'll die of thirst, yeah, then, of course.

150
00:10:26,842 --> 00:10:28,282
[SPEAKER_01]: But he'd had no such intention.

151
00:10:29,523 --> 00:10:30,963
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why intention is the action.

152
00:10:31,923 --> 00:10:36,665
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot judge the action, the outcome of action, the severity of action, without ignoring the intention behind.

153
00:10:39,518 --> 00:10:47,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the one we mentioned in previous talk, the monk who accidentally sat on that bed, like with pillars, and there was a baby underneath and killed it, broke its neck or something.

154
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:53,020
[SPEAKER_01]: And everybody said, okay, well, next time if you sit down without checking where you're sitting, it's an offense of wrongdoing.

155
00:10:54,621 --> 00:11:01,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Although that wrongdoing resulted in death of a baby, but it wasn't intentional.

156
00:11:01,762 --> 00:11:02,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So intention is that factor.

157
00:11:05,083 --> 00:11:07,143
[SPEAKER_01]: If you choose to act with intention of ill-will,

158
00:11:08,685 --> 00:11:16,631
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire, carelessness, means you're accepting that contradiction, that value, the contradicting value.

159
00:11:18,993 --> 00:11:27,159
[SPEAKER_01]: You're choosing to go down that way, because it's too unpleasant to resist it, pretty much.

160
00:11:27,579 --> 00:11:28,720
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's step back.

161
00:11:29,541 --> 00:11:37,206
[SPEAKER_00]: That precept of refraining from sexual misconduct or having sexual relations with

162
00:11:38,703 --> 00:11:41,445
[SPEAKER_00]: People you shouldn't, you know, another's wife or...

163
00:11:41,485 --> 00:11:44,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Protected, underage, all these things, yeah.

164
00:11:44,308 --> 00:11:54,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Just taking again, misappropriating things which are... Yeah, so you perceive it, you perceive it generally as not yours, as shouldn't be engaged with.

165
00:11:55,597 --> 00:12:00,581
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you choose to give priority to your desire and ignore the factual basis of your perception.

166
00:12:00,861 --> 00:12:03,683
[SPEAKER_00]: You give priority to your desires, not the others.

167
00:12:04,944 --> 00:12:09,328
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mine is first, mine is more important, mine is more valuable.

168
00:12:09,388 --> 00:12:17,415
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why even passion, even if it's passion within the precepts, even if it's sort of annoyance and ill will, but you don't break the precepts.

169
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:19,962
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where this contradiction is already rooted.

170
00:12:20,122 --> 00:12:26,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when it comes to precepts, it's just a further manifestation of you acting out of that contradiction.

171
00:12:26,308 --> 00:12:36,459
[SPEAKER_01]: But passion in itself, desire in itself, ill-will in itself, delusion or dullness in itself, that's in itself, that's already that contradiction.

172
00:12:36,719 --> 00:12:40,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why these things are unwholesome and these things always result in unwholesome.

173
00:12:41,859 --> 00:12:43,680
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you take the precepts...

174
00:12:43,700 --> 00:12:45,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is passion contradiction?

175
00:12:46,200 --> 00:12:47,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is a desire a contradiction?

176
00:12:51,842 --> 00:12:53,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Individually, from your own point of view.

177
00:12:54,362 --> 00:12:55,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire towards anything.

178
00:12:55,603 --> 00:13:01,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that desire an existential contradiction or contradiction on an existential level?

179
00:13:01,264 --> 00:13:02,425
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the discrepancy there?

180
00:13:02,505 --> 00:13:03,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you want something.

181
00:13:03,925 --> 00:13:04,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

182
00:13:04,585 --> 00:13:06,646
[SPEAKER_00]: That you cannot have.

183
00:13:07,386 --> 00:13:09,107
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I mean, you can have things.

184
00:13:09,347 --> 00:13:10,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not yours.

185
00:13:11,194 --> 00:13:12,415
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you can't have it.

186
00:13:12,835 --> 00:13:13,456
[SPEAKER_01]: You just took it.

187
00:13:14,957 --> 00:13:16,799
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not necessarily a contradiction.

188
00:13:17,799 --> 00:13:24,745
[SPEAKER_01]: On a more fundamental level, why is it contradictory to have a desire, even if you don't go and take it, even if you don't break the precepts?

189
00:13:25,385 --> 00:13:27,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it contradictory to have a desire?

190
00:13:27,827 --> 00:13:35,413
[SPEAKER_01]: To cultivate a desire, to welcome a desire, to delight in a desire?

191
00:13:38,096 --> 00:13:39,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, desire is always

192
00:13:40,547 --> 00:13:43,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards sense objects.

193
00:13:43,830 --> 00:13:47,493
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot have a desire that's not towards sense objects.

194
00:13:47,533 --> 00:13:49,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts.

195
00:13:49,635 --> 00:13:51,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It always has... it's inconceivable.

196
00:13:51,737 --> 00:13:55,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever you desire, it's going to be one of these six things.

197
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:57,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, thoughts.

198
00:13:58,923 --> 00:14:03,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have any of these six things without the senses being there?

199
00:14:04,768 --> 00:14:05,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Without the mind being there?

200
00:14:06,670 --> 00:14:08,131
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have sights without the eye?

201
00:14:09,592 --> 00:14:10,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Sounds without ears.

202
00:14:10,712 --> 00:14:11,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

203
00:14:12,073 --> 00:14:18,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the nature of the eye, ear, nose, body, tongue, mind?

204
00:14:18,894 --> 00:14:19,674
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the nature of it?

205
00:14:20,495 --> 00:14:21,315
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

206
00:14:22,175 --> 00:14:29,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you then cultivate a desire towards that which you can only get on account of things that are not yours?

207
00:14:29,117 --> 00:14:33,178
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way you can cultivate desire towards those things is to take these things to be yours to begin with.

208
00:14:33,918 --> 00:14:39,299
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's impossible for you to have a desire towards sense objects without implicitly taking the ownership of the senses.

209
00:14:40,200 --> 00:14:40,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's what I...

210
00:14:41,163 --> 00:14:43,649
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought I was saying that, actually, but...

211
00:14:43,669 --> 00:14:48,683
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't... you might have been saying it in general terms, but I didn't kind of see that you were saying it on the level of...

212
00:14:50,183 --> 00:15:02,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't act, even if you don't break the precepts, even if you don't do anything, having a desire that is being welcomed and delighted in and accepted and not understood, is already that existential contradiction we talk about.

213
00:15:02,993 --> 00:15:15,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you will not be able to welcome a desire, entertain it, even if you don't act out of it, without that entertainment of that desire implicitly taking up the sense bases as yours.

214
00:15:16,643 --> 00:15:17,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:15:17,503 --> 00:15:18,864
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking up Mara's domain.

216
00:15:19,264 --> 00:15:19,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

217
00:15:19,704 --> 00:15:19,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

218
00:15:20,064 --> 00:15:21,625
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I was going to say just now.

219
00:15:22,105 --> 00:15:29,548
[SPEAKER_00]: The more such the precept of not acting out of sensuality, you know, being celibate.

220
00:15:29,588 --> 00:15:30,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure, sure.

221
00:15:30,068 --> 00:15:32,669
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're no longer taking these senses to be yours.

222
00:15:33,889 --> 00:15:34,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

223
00:15:35,710 --> 00:15:45,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Not acting out of sensuality, being celibate, is the necessary sort of precondition for you to then eventually not take these senses to be yours on the level of your thought.

224
00:15:47,292 --> 00:15:48,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what Buddha said.

225
00:15:48,673 --> 00:15:55,617
[SPEAKER_01]: A monk can live withdrawn from centrality, but if he hasn't understood it, uprooted it internally, on the level of his thoughts, he's still bound by it.

226
00:15:56,237 --> 00:16:06,043
[SPEAKER_01]: And then gave the simile, like, you can step outside of the water, and get the sticks to light the fire, but because they're still wet because of that water, you won't light the fire.

227
00:16:06,143 --> 00:16:09,005
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to wait for them to dry up, and not keep going back to the water.

228
00:16:09,995 --> 00:16:18,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to live withdrawn from centrality physically, as a celibate, but then also mentally you need to start withdrawing your mind so it dries up from that centrality.

229
00:16:18,279 --> 00:16:20,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can stop taking that which is not yours.

230
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:21,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

231
00:16:21,181 --> 00:16:25,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Because taking up what's not yours starts on the level of welcoming a desire.

232
00:16:25,623 --> 00:16:26,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire arises.

233
00:16:26,744 --> 00:16:28,264
[SPEAKER_01]: By welcoming it, you take it up.

234
00:16:28,765 --> 00:16:33,247
[SPEAKER_01]: When you take up desire, you take every value that that desire implies.

235
00:16:33,707 --> 00:16:36,408
[SPEAKER_01]: The value is that senses are in your control.

236
00:16:36,928 --> 00:16:38,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Senses can be enjoyed.

237
00:16:38,228 --> 00:16:39,949
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not dangerous to enjoy the senses.

238
00:16:40,009 --> 00:16:42,229
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not dangerous to pursue whatever they take you.

239
00:16:42,249 --> 00:16:45,630
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're acting from a premise that these senses are mine.

240
00:16:45,870 --> 00:16:46,090
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

241
00:16:46,430 --> 00:16:49,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense experience is safe, secure.

242
00:16:49,691 --> 00:16:50,551
[SPEAKER_01]: All that's implicit.

243
00:16:50,571 --> 00:16:50,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

244
00:16:50,751 --> 00:16:51,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Lovely.

245
00:16:51,251 --> 00:16:51,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

246
00:16:51,912 --> 00:16:53,172
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is completely implicit.

247
00:16:53,192 --> 00:16:54,432
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even, you don't even think about it.

248
00:16:54,452 --> 00:16:57,553
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that whole delighting and so on is already... That's because he doesn't delight.

249
00:16:57,573 --> 00:17:00,914
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say desire to not manifest itself.

250
00:17:02,929 --> 00:17:08,515
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in that, but you have a say in your attitude towards the manifestation of desire, or your will, for that matter.

251
00:17:09,136 --> 00:17:09,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I welcoming?

252
00:17:09,996 --> 00:17:11,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I relishing this?

253
00:17:11,418 --> 00:17:15,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I kind of rejoicing in the prospect of desire and then fulfilling that desire?

254
00:17:15,883 --> 00:17:22,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if then I practice sense restraint, am I still entertaining a phenomenon of desire in my experience when desire manifests?

255
00:17:24,339 --> 00:17:27,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do, that's why you're still contradicting yourself on the existential basis.

256
00:17:28,081 --> 00:17:36,705
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you cannot possibly entertain, as I said, the value of desire, without implicitly taking up the ownership of the sense bases.

257
00:17:38,606 --> 00:17:49,851
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop valuing the sense objects, stop entertaining desire towards them, you get to uproot taking up the sense bases as yours, the sense organs as yours.

258
00:17:50,371 --> 00:17:51,372
[SPEAKER_00]: You have desire,

259
00:17:52,798 --> 00:17:58,059
[SPEAKER_00]: Towards sense objects, because you've misappropriated the sense senses.

260
00:17:58,079 --> 00:17:59,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's like a vicious circle.

261
00:17:59,899 --> 00:18:06,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because you appropriate the senses, as in, you don't question your ownership of them, of your eye, nose, ear, the body.

262
00:18:07,401 --> 00:18:09,982
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you cultivate desire towards the sense objects.

263
00:18:10,382 --> 00:18:14,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you cultivate desire towards sense objects, that's how you appropriate the sense organs.

264
00:18:14,983 --> 00:18:16,283
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one feeds the other.

265
00:18:17,854 --> 00:18:25,583
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first you need to stop... first you need to start restraining your behavior on account of the desire towards sense objects, as in you need to start keeping the precepts.

266
00:18:27,004 --> 00:18:33,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, once you're established in that, once that becomes your norm of behavior,

267
00:18:34,907 --> 00:18:44,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can get to see that on the mental level, you should now stop delighting in it, welcoming it, sort of flirting it in your mind and accepting it.

268
00:18:44,811 --> 00:18:50,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you know you're not gonna act physically, now you make the further effort and don't welcome it with the mind, don't engage in it.

269
00:18:51,153 --> 00:18:58,155
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eventually, through not... basically, you will dry out of that wetness, of that desire, so to speak.

270
00:18:58,195 --> 00:19:00,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get to see the true nature of those senses.

271
00:19:00,456 --> 00:19:01,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh my goodness, this is peril.

272
00:19:01,536 --> 00:19:10,239
[SPEAKER_01]: This is like... Each time I was engaging with the desire towards sense objects, it was like putting my head on a chopping block, which is what the sense organ is.

273
00:19:10,279 --> 00:19:12,219
[SPEAKER_01]: And the axe could have fallen at any given time.

274
00:19:12,379 --> 00:19:13,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Balancing on this...

275
00:19:16,105 --> 00:19:24,558
[SPEAKER_01]: This insecure thing... Well, yeah, you basically, because of your desire to sense organs, you go into a trap.

276
00:19:24,878 --> 00:19:25,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:19:25,499 --> 00:19:29,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, because of your desire to sense objects, you go into the trap of sense organs.

278
00:19:32,282 --> 00:19:35,805
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... we've done that... the simile... the lion's reserve.

279
00:19:36,325 --> 00:19:37,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

280
00:19:37,927 --> 00:19:39,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Lion's enclosure.

281
00:19:39,528 --> 00:19:51,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are making yourself... like, you're exposing yourself to this danger, to this peril, to this risk, to death, deadly suffering, because of your desires towards sense objects, which implies the ownership of sense organs.

282
00:19:52,758 --> 00:19:59,984
[SPEAKER_01]: And sense organs are that chopping block, are that... are that... are that trap, and lion's den, and so on, that you walk into.

283
00:20:03,798 --> 00:20:11,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't just say, okay, I'm not regarding my senses as mine anymore, because that thought of your senses, it's basically a sense object.

284
00:20:11,843 --> 00:20:12,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

285
00:20:12,723 --> 00:20:19,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot directly experience your sense organs, which is why there's always that implicit ownership that you need to undo indirectly.

286
00:20:19,888 --> 00:20:23,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just decide, I'll let go of this, because you're not holding it intentionally.

287
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:26,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is... what else do you need to let go?

288
00:20:26,820 --> 00:20:32,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, what is the only other thing that you... if you let go, then you will not have the ownership of the sense organs?

289
00:20:32,602 --> 00:20:34,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, it's the desire to sense objects.

290
00:20:34,922 --> 00:20:38,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Let go of that, you step closer to then let go of the sense organs.

291
00:20:40,343 --> 00:20:45,604
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you think you're letting go of sense organs, you're regarding your own sense organs as sense objects.

292
00:20:46,104 --> 00:20:53,306
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not being overly into perceptions,

293
00:20:54,224 --> 00:20:54,945
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean by that?

294
00:20:56,628 --> 00:21:08,325
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're not delighting it, you're not focusing and you're not entangled within your perceptions, you can let go of them, so to speak, to step back to see what's behind there, what they are based upon.

295
00:21:10,335 --> 00:21:14,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but like, when you see, when you want to see what's behind, it's not like another thing you want to see.

296
00:21:14,457 --> 00:21:21,980
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, you mustn't act out of it, out of that delight and desire, and then you need to understand it, understand that desire.

297
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,342
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing else you need to sort of psychologize out of it, to figure it out.

298
00:21:26,802 --> 00:21:31,344
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to understand the nature of that thing, which is right there, that desire.

299
00:21:31,764 --> 00:21:32,824
[SPEAKER_01]: The peril, the danger.

300
00:21:33,104 --> 00:21:34,485
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying it's implicit.

301
00:21:35,085 --> 00:21:38,087
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not found in response to this desire or after.

302
00:21:38,127 --> 00:21:38,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in it.

303
00:21:38,768 --> 00:21:40,189
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is in every desire.

304
00:21:40,309 --> 00:21:45,313
[SPEAKER_01]: In every time you delight in any desire of any kind, all these things are implicit.

305
00:21:45,413 --> 00:21:47,214
[SPEAKER_01]: All these universal values are implicit.

306
00:21:49,276 --> 00:21:49,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

307
00:21:49,836 --> 00:21:55,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the eye cannot see itself, literally.

308
00:21:56,061 --> 00:21:58,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense organs cannot see itself as a sense object.

309
00:22:00,144 --> 00:22:02,485
[SPEAKER_01]: or of its corresponding nature.

310
00:22:02,665 --> 00:22:03,306
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

311
00:22:04,046 --> 00:22:04,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Utterly impossible.

312
00:22:05,107 --> 00:22:08,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you think you're letting go of your eyes, of your body, you're not.

313
00:22:09,809 --> 00:22:14,412
[SPEAKER_01]: You're basically... that thought is misconceiving sense objects to be the sense organ.

314
00:22:14,492 --> 00:22:19,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, you can imagine an eye, but that's not the eye because of which you have sights.

315
00:22:20,449 --> 00:22:31,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to abandon the I, because of which... to abandon the ownership of I, because of which you have sights, is to see the peril of the desire towards sights, the danger to not delight in it.

316
00:22:31,857 --> 00:22:43,866
[SPEAKER_01]: When you've sufficiently seen that, the mind will withdraw itself from the domain of sights, and by doing so, implicitly, withdraw that ownership of the domain of sights as I, the sense object of the sights.

317
00:22:45,267 --> 00:22:47,548
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a trap.

318
00:22:47,629 --> 00:22:49,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You are lured by something else.

319
00:22:50,698 --> 00:22:52,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Driven by something else, and you walk into a trap.

320
00:22:52,979 --> 00:22:53,919
[SPEAKER_01]: You never see the trap.

321
00:22:54,319 --> 00:22:55,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't trap you.

322
00:22:57,461 --> 00:22:57,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

323
00:22:57,881 --> 00:23:02,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you withdraw yourself from the particular bait that traps you, and that's how you escape the trap.

324
00:23:03,343 --> 00:23:05,124
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't see the trap, but you can see the bait.

325
00:23:06,144 --> 00:23:06,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the bait?

326
00:23:07,084 --> 00:23:09,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire in regard to sights is the Mara's bait.

327
00:23:11,106 --> 00:23:11,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

328
00:23:12,446 --> 00:23:14,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you remove the desire towards sights,

329
00:23:15,645 --> 00:23:21,834
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no entering the trap of any kind, so you don't need to worry about the, like, specificity of a trap.

330
00:23:22,474 --> 00:23:23,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Hmm, this is how it works.

331
00:23:23,456 --> 00:23:24,557
[SPEAKER_01]: When I think this, they say, no.

332
00:23:25,539 --> 00:23:30,125
[SPEAKER_01]: No desire towards sights, no trap of any kind that you can walk into.

333
00:23:34,840 --> 00:23:41,665
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm trying to think what's the next precept of the... Third... Did we do the third?

334
00:23:41,685 --> 00:23:43,066
[SPEAKER_01]: We did, yeah.

335
00:23:44,127 --> 00:23:45,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's the lying, isn't it?

336
00:23:45,908 --> 00:23:46,829
[SPEAKER_00]: I said lying already.

337
00:23:47,089 --> 00:23:48,310
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, I don't... No, it was feeling.

338
00:23:48,370 --> 00:23:49,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what I thought it was.

339
00:23:49,951 --> 00:23:54,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, lying is misrepresenting the truth.

340
00:23:54,655 --> 00:23:54,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

341
00:23:55,656 --> 00:23:56,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally.

342
00:23:56,216 --> 00:23:56,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

343
00:23:57,077 --> 00:24:03,542
[SPEAKER_00]: But, fundamentally, you are... you are living concealment.

344
00:24:04,040 --> 00:24:04,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

345
00:24:05,240 --> 00:24:15,043
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the biggest lie, which you kind of... Yeah, the biggest lie is that the fundamental lie of ignorance.

346
00:24:15,263 --> 00:24:17,423
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to use the word ignorance necessarily.

347
00:24:18,203 --> 00:24:23,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Of avijjā, the fundamental lie of delusion, dullness.

348
00:24:24,885 --> 00:24:28,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, would you be able, for example,

349
00:24:29,609 --> 00:24:37,611
[SPEAKER_01]: To be complacent, lazy, dull and careless, if you're placed in a dangerous situation.

350
00:24:38,471 --> 00:24:39,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Did you perceive the danger of it?

351
00:24:39,912 --> 00:24:40,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

352
00:24:40,572 --> 00:24:49,074
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be inconceivable for you to relax on that level, to like completely just let your mind kind of melt away and forget about itself.

353
00:24:50,414 --> 00:24:53,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are, in the most perilous situation you can be,

354
00:24:55,500 --> 00:25:00,523
[SPEAKER_01]: With death, sickness, aging, everything else is approaching elements from all the directions.

355
00:25:00,543 --> 00:25:01,363
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't escape it.

356
00:25:01,443 --> 00:25:04,505
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are, completely complacent.

357
00:25:04,545 --> 00:25:05,905
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the fundamental contradiction.

358
00:25:05,925 --> 00:25:06,946
[SPEAKER_01]: The hardest one to undo.

359
00:25:08,187 --> 00:25:10,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not as obvious as desire or as it will.

360
00:25:10,728 --> 00:25:12,209
[SPEAKER_00]: There is danger, but you just don't see it.

361
00:25:13,049 --> 00:25:13,730
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's implied.

362
00:25:14,010 --> 00:25:14,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel it.

363
00:25:14,510 --> 00:25:15,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

364
00:25:15,190 --> 00:25:17,111
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like the danger doesn't exist.

365
00:25:17,652 --> 00:25:23,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Just for example, if you if you want to do a little experiment, if you're not sure if it's dangerous or not, your existence in itself,

366
00:25:24,882 --> 00:25:29,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Just lock yourself in a room, comfortable room, but don't engage with any distraction for a day.

367
00:25:31,347 --> 00:25:38,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Switch your phone off, switch everything, don't talk to anyone, don't step outside, just sit alone on a chair or a bed, whatever else, and stare in the wall.

368
00:25:39,292 --> 00:25:44,116
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... let's see how long you can do that before you start freaking out on account of that boredom.

369
00:25:45,278 --> 00:25:48,179
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if boredom is not truly dangerous, why are you so afraid of it?

370
00:25:48,699 --> 00:25:53,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you constantly need something to keep you engaged, to swim, so you don't sink deep down?

371
00:25:53,840 --> 00:25:55,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you terrified of solitude and loneliness?

372
00:25:56,600 --> 00:25:59,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Which, like, 99.99% people are.

373
00:25:59,461 --> 00:26:04,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you need to make the effort to basically develop your mind and wisdom in order to be able to delight in solitude.

374
00:26:06,502 --> 00:26:11,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you realize that, oh, if I'm... Say if I get sick and I can't move.

375
00:26:12,922 --> 00:26:17,667
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can't distract myself with all these things and projects I have to do.

376
00:26:17,727 --> 00:26:18,809
[SPEAKER_01]: How will my mind remain?

377
00:26:19,469 --> 00:26:20,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Will it be unaffected by this?

378
00:26:21,191 --> 00:26:24,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Or will I start completely losing it and freaking out?

379
00:26:25,967 --> 00:26:27,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, boredom.

380
00:26:27,247 --> 00:26:28,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It's so, I'm bored.

381
00:26:28,768 --> 00:26:33,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, let the boredom endure now, and it's going to turn into full-blown anxiety and dread.

382
00:26:33,769 --> 00:26:37,330
[SPEAKER_01]: And why is that, if existence is not dangerous and perilous?

383
00:26:37,710 --> 00:26:39,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does this anxiety come from?

384
00:26:39,111 --> 00:26:40,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't done anything, by the way.

385
00:26:41,251 --> 00:26:42,872
[SPEAKER_01]: You're in a perfectly safe environment.

386
00:26:44,052 --> 00:26:44,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Locked.

387
00:26:44,352 --> 00:26:45,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can get in.

388
00:26:45,573 --> 00:26:46,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Safe as you can be.

389
00:26:46,793 --> 00:26:48,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are freaking out internally.

390
00:26:49,914 --> 00:26:51,394
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because that's what existence is.

391
00:26:52,294 --> 00:26:54,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all these efforts and actions you do,

392
00:26:55,613 --> 00:26:58,254
[SPEAKER_01]: To enable yourself to ignore that very nature.

393
00:26:59,035 --> 00:27:05,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why delusion, like lobha, dosa, moha, the three kind of unwholesome outpours, that's why they're unwholesome.

394
00:27:06,719 --> 00:27:08,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion being the third one, or dullness.

395
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:11,521
[SPEAKER_01]: That dullness is a form of complacency.

396
00:27:12,662 --> 00:27:21,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And the only way you... like, you cannot be complacent without implicit kind of... without implicitly sort of assuming or ignoring the fact

397
00:27:22,380 --> 00:27:23,260
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's not dangerous.

398
00:27:24,121 --> 00:27:25,441
[SPEAKER_01]: That existence is not perilous.

399
00:27:26,802 --> 00:27:35,024
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just assume safety, because you are complacent, and because you assume safety, you make the effort toward complacency.

400
00:27:35,184 --> 00:27:38,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Same like with that, what we said, with the desire and ownership.

401
00:27:38,425 --> 00:27:39,166
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the best thing.

402
00:27:39,306 --> 00:27:42,687
[SPEAKER_00]: One has to understand that anicca is dukkha.

403
00:27:43,342 --> 00:27:44,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, anicca is dukkha.

404
00:27:44,843 --> 00:27:55,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And by seeing that dukkha, because of... By seeing anicca, and by seeing anicca is dukkha, you're then implicitly withdrawing yourself from the ownership of atta, which is anatta.

405
00:27:55,832 --> 00:27:59,435
[SPEAKER_00]: But that seeing dukkha means that you are no longer, yeah, complacent.

406
00:27:59,595 --> 00:28:00,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot be complacent.

407
00:28:01,256 --> 00:28:06,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to see that dukkha, and the dukkha of boredom is the dukkha of this is, the dukkha of uneasiness.

408
00:28:06,581 --> 00:28:07,781
[SPEAKER_00]: There's so many ways.

409
00:28:08,562 --> 00:28:13,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing that dukkha is like seeing your executioner with his sword drawn out.

410
00:28:13,964 --> 00:28:15,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Waiting for you.

411
00:28:15,145 --> 00:28:15,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there.

412
00:28:15,645 --> 00:28:16,365
[SPEAKER_01]: He's waiting for you.

413
00:28:16,485 --> 00:28:20,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have that... you can't go... you can't relax and just watch a movie now.

414
00:28:21,107 --> 00:28:23,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

415
00:28:23,448 --> 00:28:26,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like somebody comes and tells you, I have 10 minutes to live.

416
00:28:26,769 --> 00:28:27,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thank you.

417
00:28:27,450 --> 00:28:31,211
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just carry on watching this movie and laughing carelessly.

418
00:28:31,651 --> 00:28:33,092
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be impossible for you to do that.

419
00:28:34,215 --> 00:28:37,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Suddenly, the sense of urgency is back on.

420
00:28:37,801 --> 00:28:42,069
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason sense of urgency can be kind of restored is because it's always there implicit.

421
00:28:43,970 --> 00:28:49,552
[SPEAKER_01]: That anxiety, that thread of anxiety, that you can never completely cover up, is what human existence is, for everyone.

422
00:28:50,252 --> 00:28:51,752
[SPEAKER_01]: People who practice, people who don't practice.

423
00:28:51,852 --> 00:28:52,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody can outrun it.

424
00:28:53,713 --> 00:28:55,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Only Arhat can free himself from it completely.

425
00:28:56,194 --> 00:29:05,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, by trapping yourself into a room or whatever... Yeah, so... but when you are, like, young, healthy, you have all the senses working, everything at your disposal, it feels like there's no problem.

426
00:29:06,497 --> 00:29:08,019
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, just do a little experiment.

427
00:29:08,059 --> 00:29:15,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Close yourself in for like half a day and see what's gonna start happening to your own mind when there are no distractions available.

428
00:29:16,269 --> 00:29:22,297
[SPEAKER_01]: And if your mind was problem-free beforehand, this would not cause any issues.

429
00:29:22,677 --> 00:29:23,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it does.

430
00:29:23,578 --> 00:29:26,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Solitude starts basically... Because you're revealing what's actually there.

431
00:29:26,741 --> 00:29:27,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

432
00:29:27,081 --> 00:29:39,733
[SPEAKER_01]: You're taking away... Well, you're revealing what's your main motivator for all this engagement with life, is to cover up the fact that it's perilous and underlying anxiety that sort of means you're not in control.

433
00:29:39,873 --> 00:29:42,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, as you said before, the whole practice

434
00:29:43,757 --> 00:29:46,580
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about unconcealing things, or in a suit, as it says.

435
00:29:46,600 --> 00:29:46,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

436
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:47,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Undoing.

437
00:29:47,400 --> 00:29:49,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Removing this and concealing that which is covered.

438
00:29:49,362 --> 00:29:49,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncovering.

439
00:29:49,682 --> 00:29:57,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, there's a way to say, like a skilled butcher gets the first, you know, the skin off, the meat, the tendons, sinews, everything uncovering.

440
00:29:57,849 --> 00:30:00,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like analyzing on an abstract level, which is sometimes how people take it.

441
00:30:01,112 --> 00:30:01,372
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

442
00:30:01,532 --> 00:30:06,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It means uncovering all the layers of a cover-up until you get to the core.

443
00:30:09,199 --> 00:30:14,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Just... fifth precept on... Intoxicants.

444
00:30:14,703 --> 00:30:15,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's exactly it.

445
00:30:16,003 --> 00:30:25,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you possibly engage with intoxicating substances without implicitly endorsing complacency and dullness of your mind?

446
00:30:26,049 --> 00:30:26,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

447
00:30:27,090 --> 00:30:28,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

448
00:30:28,411 --> 00:30:29,131
[SPEAKER_00]: Just have fun.

449
00:30:29,692 --> 00:30:30,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

450
00:30:31,393 --> 00:30:33,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's just like, you know, I don't necessarily get drunk.

451
00:30:33,854 --> 00:30:34,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

452
00:30:34,595 --> 00:30:36,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging with that substance, to some extent,

453
00:30:38,213 --> 00:30:39,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Is increasing dullness of your mind.

454
00:30:40,135 --> 00:30:41,516
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

455
00:30:41,536 --> 00:30:42,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Of heedlessness.

456
00:30:42,637 --> 00:30:48,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless there is no alcohol in the substance, which always results in increasing... Well, dullness means heedlessness.

457
00:30:48,242 --> 00:30:51,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Dullness means loss of context, loss of perspective.

458
00:30:51,684 --> 00:30:59,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's for a grain a bit, like not as much as you're completely drunk, it's still the direction of losing context and perspective, even if it's half a step.

459
00:31:00,532 --> 00:31:02,173
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still that same thing.

460
00:31:02,594 --> 00:31:03,574
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the Buddha said, same.

461
00:31:04,600 --> 00:31:07,944
[SPEAKER_01]: A pound of excrement is equally disgusting as a little grain of it.

462
00:31:08,545 --> 00:31:11,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Smells horrible, looks horrible, and tastes horrible.

463
00:31:12,650 --> 00:31:18,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that you engage a little bit, maybe, oh, I only take a glass of wine, I don't get drunk, doesn't matter.

464
00:31:19,379 --> 00:31:21,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I only take a scoop of excrement.

465
00:31:22,618 --> 00:31:25,480
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the result of dullness, even if it's miniscule.

466
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:31,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental dullness is still the result of your action, which means you still value it, you're still engaging with it.

467
00:31:32,125 --> 00:31:50,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you engage with even like a sort of absolute minimal level, so to speak, of intoxication, just tiny bit, without valuing, without delighting at the prospect of dullness of the mind?

468
00:31:51,350 --> 00:31:52,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Preferring that.

469
00:31:52,131 --> 00:31:52,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

470
00:31:52,411 --> 00:31:55,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Numbness, dullness, distraction, any of it.

471
00:31:56,213 --> 00:32:07,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which means, can you... can you be engaging with that direction of intoxicating your mind even a tiny bit, without sort of not seeing the danger of it?

472
00:32:07,320 --> 00:32:09,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to engage with it if you see it as dangerous?

473
00:32:10,562 --> 00:32:12,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I will not go all the way into the lion's den.

474
00:32:12,984 --> 00:32:14,365
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just set the foot inside.

475
00:32:14,945 --> 00:32:17,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but what if the lion is right there by the entrance?

476
00:32:17,447 --> 00:32:18,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is a chance that it might be.

477
00:32:19,838 --> 00:32:20,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you wouldn't do it.

478
00:32:20,539 --> 00:32:22,200
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't flirt with that idea at all.

479
00:32:23,441 --> 00:32:24,301
[SPEAKER_00]: A little bit of poison.

480
00:32:24,522 --> 00:32:24,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

481
00:32:24,882 --> 00:32:26,623
[SPEAKER_01]: A little bit of poison is still a poison.

482
00:32:27,864 --> 00:32:28,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't affect you.

483
00:32:28,705 --> 00:32:34,709
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a lot of poison, but it's still a poison, which means it still affects you, which means that might be enough in itself.

484
00:32:38,577 --> 00:32:46,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're already covered, like you're already full of wrong views in regard to everything, in regard to desire, in regard to ill-will, in regard to dullness.

485
00:32:47,260 --> 00:32:51,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's already like a mountain of work for all of that to be undone, as we said.

486
00:32:52,322 --> 00:32:56,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, here you are flirting with the things that directly result in increasing of those wrong views.

487
00:32:58,594 --> 00:33:09,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, literally, a glass of wine might be enough for you to fully cement you in your wrong views that sensuality is not bad or not see it as dangerous enough.

488
00:33:09,760 --> 00:33:14,822
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that, you will not get the right view and you will remain bound to saṃsāra for eons to come.

489
00:33:15,583 --> 00:33:18,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the glass of wine really worth that risk?

490
00:33:19,421 --> 00:33:21,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you really know that that risk is not implied?

491
00:33:21,803 --> 00:33:22,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the slightest clue.

492
00:33:23,304 --> 00:33:25,606
[SPEAKER_01]: You just wishful thinking and believe in it.

493
00:33:26,186 --> 00:33:32,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, you perceive that it's not as bad as two liters of wine, but it doesn't mean that it's not bad.

494
00:33:34,353 --> 00:33:35,894
[SPEAKER_00]: It's based on the same thing.

495
00:33:36,034 --> 00:33:36,855
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same thing.

496
00:33:38,316 --> 00:33:41,798
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a whole... it had taken intoxicants.

497
00:33:41,838 --> 00:33:46,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what they were... sorry, there was that case of a man with the right view in the sutta, who was drinking.

498
00:33:48,832 --> 00:33:55,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Who wasn't practicing, he got a right view, then stopped practicing, because he was happy with the right view, and then got careless in his precepts.

499
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:04,185
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when he was basically dying, he undertook the training again, and pretty much stopped drinking.

500
00:34:04,265 --> 00:34:05,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's like for a day, it doesn't matter.

501
00:34:06,346 --> 00:34:07,567
[SPEAKER_01]: He had the basis of right view.

502
00:34:08,028 --> 00:34:10,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So the Buddha said, well, for him, it was different, because why?

503
00:34:10,810 --> 00:34:11,691
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

504
00:34:11,771 --> 00:34:15,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if he was absolutely pissing drunk, every single day of his life,

505
00:34:17,198 --> 00:34:21,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Having developed the right view beforehand, he cannot develop the wrong views now.

506
00:34:21,526 --> 00:34:27,317
[SPEAKER_01]: He's certainly not pursuing the direction of the right view that results in arahantship, but he cannot undo the right view.

507
00:34:28,448 --> 00:34:36,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the amount of drinking, the amount of engaging in breaking the precepts, cannot proliferate his wrong views, which is exactly what happens to somebody without the right view.

508
00:34:36,755 --> 00:34:42,439
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's also exactly why it's very unlikely that somebody with the right view would break those precepts like that.

509
00:34:43,059 --> 00:34:49,084
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, yeah, there is always exception to every rule, and that's why two and a half thousand years later, we're still talking about that man.

510
00:34:49,404 --> 00:34:50,865
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he made it into the suttas.

511
00:34:51,125 --> 00:34:51,766
[SPEAKER_01]: He stood out.

512
00:34:53,487 --> 00:34:55,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of them would not do that.

513
00:34:55,389 --> 00:34:57,751
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for whatever reasons, he had his reasons.

514
00:34:58,331 --> 00:34:59,792
[SPEAKER_01]: One in a million has done that.

515
00:35:00,233 --> 00:35:08,560
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, you use that one example, pretty much anecdotal evidence, to say that you, without the right view, is fine to drink, because sotāpanna can drink.

516
00:35:09,020 --> 00:35:10,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so drinking, you can match that.

517
00:35:11,162 --> 00:35:14,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Why don't you match the right view of a sotāpanna as well, and then see how you can drink?

518
00:35:15,326 --> 00:35:16,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Or how you can break the precepts?

519
00:35:17,067 --> 00:35:20,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, theoretically you could, but not until you get the right view first.

520
00:35:22,136 --> 00:35:24,837
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm just explaining, like, how it did not affect him.

521
00:35:25,737 --> 00:35:51,585
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it affected him, obviously, because he could have become an arahant in this life, but it did not affect him in the same sense as somebody without a right view, even just not seeing the danger in the tiny little step towards intoxication and dullness, might be more than enough for fully solidifying wrong views that you should have been undoing, which means you will not... you will not get the right view as a result of you lacking development on the... in the domain of precepts.

522
00:35:54,245 --> 00:35:56,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And even, like, we proposed to that before.

523
00:35:57,326 --> 00:36:12,268
[SPEAKER_01]: The suttas were never... even when the Buddha was teaching lay people, before they would get the right view, he would start teaching them the, like, you know, benefits of virtue, generosity, and then the peril of sensuality, how it's of little value, little satisfaction.

524
00:36:12,808 --> 00:36:16,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the mind would be primed like that, then he would teach them, and then they would get to understand.

525
00:36:16,489 --> 00:36:23,450
[SPEAKER_01]: And then they might go back to their wives and husbands and so on, but they can't undo what they understood, which means they'll never be able to be as careless.

526
00:36:26,885 --> 00:36:32,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, if you get the right view and then choose to go back to centrality, that's your choice.

527
00:36:32,707 --> 00:36:37,389
[SPEAKER_01]: But saying, because the one with the right view can go to centrality, means I can do centrality without a right view.

528
00:36:37,409 --> 00:36:38,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's just ignorant thinking.

529
00:36:38,849 --> 00:36:40,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally ignorant thinking.

530
00:36:41,370 --> 00:36:43,011
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you obviously see that it's not the same.

531
00:36:44,925 --> 00:36:48,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's similar to intoxication.

532
00:36:48,409 --> 00:36:51,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Intoxication with life, intoxication with health, intoxication with...

533
00:36:52,593 --> 00:36:54,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah, of course, of course, of course.

534
00:36:54,195 --> 00:36:55,957
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean, losing the context.

535
00:36:56,377 --> 00:37:04,586
[SPEAKER_01]: That still context is that, oh, yeah, I might be healthy and alive and young now, but can I guarantee that there will be the next in and out breath?

536
00:37:04,646 --> 00:37:07,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll be a lot... No, you have absolutely no guarantee of that.

537
00:37:07,790 --> 00:37:09,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you don't think about that, that's...

538
00:37:09,610 --> 00:37:18,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you fall away from recognizing that context, then you start heading into the direction of the desire towards sense objects, which is intoxication as well.

539
00:37:18,693 --> 00:37:22,134
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the more subtle application of that precept.

540
00:37:22,194 --> 00:37:23,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, sure.

541
00:37:23,254 --> 00:37:24,314
[SPEAKER_00]: You go deeper there.

542
00:37:24,414 --> 00:37:27,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking no intoxicants, no longer intoxicated.

543
00:37:27,435 --> 00:37:30,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but I don't get drunk when I drink one glass of wine or one beer.

544
00:37:31,016 --> 00:37:32,496
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're intoxicated with life.

545
00:37:32,636 --> 00:37:33,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Being drunk is not a measure.

546
00:37:34,917 --> 00:37:36,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Of whether you're wholesome or unwholesome.

547
00:37:36,838 --> 00:37:39,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Engaging with the unwholesome direction is a measure.

548
00:37:40,679 --> 00:37:47,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter if you engage ten steps into the unwholesome direction on one step, you're accepting the direction of unwholesome full stop.

549
00:37:49,462 --> 00:37:53,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's this again, like, common sort of pututjana's wisdom.

550
00:37:53,243 --> 00:37:54,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't get drunk.

551
00:37:54,283 --> 00:37:56,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says you shouldn't get drunk.

552
00:37:56,604 --> 00:37:59,145
[SPEAKER_01]: You shouldn't drink alcohol, but not drinking alcohol, you cannot get drunk.

553
00:38:00,117 --> 00:38:00,737
[SPEAKER_01]: But is this...

554
00:38:00,777 --> 00:38:02,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Drunk is being the problem.

555
00:38:02,058 --> 00:38:02,218
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

556
00:38:02,498 --> 00:38:04,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Drinking alcohol is the problem.

557
00:38:04,459 --> 00:38:09,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because you get drunk, but because it's implicitly taking up of the unwholesome direction.

558
00:38:12,004 --> 00:38:13,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Even a tiny bit.

559
00:38:16,326 --> 00:38:18,487
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you won't have to be worried about whether you're drunk or not.

560
00:38:20,128 --> 00:38:22,589
[SPEAKER_01]: That was never the threshold for the precept.

561
00:38:28,172 --> 00:38:29,213
[SPEAKER_00]: It was never the threshold.

562
00:38:29,803 --> 00:38:31,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the precept doesn't say don't get drunk.

563
00:38:31,905 --> 00:38:33,466
[SPEAKER_01]: It says don't consume intoxicants.

564
00:38:35,168 --> 00:38:36,809
[SPEAKER_01]: They then result in drunkenness and so on.

565
00:38:36,829 --> 00:38:37,370
[SPEAKER_01]: But I don't get drunk.

566
00:38:37,390 --> 00:38:38,070
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, it doesn't matter.

567
00:38:38,391 --> 00:38:39,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Have you consumed intoxicants?

568
00:38:39,531 --> 00:38:39,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

569
00:38:40,432 --> 00:38:52,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to consume intoxicants without implicit value of mental dullness and relaxation, basically, on the level of ignoring the necessary context of perilous existence?

570
00:38:54,032 --> 00:38:57,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey, you're gonna die in five minutes, but let's have a glass of wine.

571
00:38:57,455 --> 00:38:58,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's forget about it.

572
00:38:58,596 --> 00:38:59,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's get drunk.

573
00:38:59,457 --> 00:39:00,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Help you deal with it.

574
00:39:00,798 --> 00:39:00,958
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

575
00:39:01,299 --> 00:39:02,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there anything I can do?

576
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:04,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I might have a chance to save myself.

577
00:39:04,241 --> 00:39:06,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you have a chance to save yourself, but forget about it.

578
00:39:07,124 --> 00:39:07,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's drink.

579
00:39:08,645 --> 00:39:09,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's enjoy ourselves.

580
00:39:09,867 --> 00:39:15,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one would you choose, even as the one who values alcohol and party and everything else?

581
00:39:15,752 --> 00:39:18,835
[SPEAKER_01]: You would choose to do something about it when you recognize that you could.

582
00:39:20,529 --> 00:39:33,453
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason now you will be accepting the direction of distraction, intoxication, relaxation on the level of mental dullness, would be because you're completely unaware of the danger.

583
00:39:33,633 --> 00:39:37,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not become aware of the danger on account of choosing the direction of unwholesome.

584
00:39:42,755 --> 00:39:45,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the other precepts of

585
00:39:48,775 --> 00:39:52,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, no entertainment, no decoration.

586
00:39:52,739 --> 00:39:53,940
[SPEAKER_01]: We're now going to eight precepts.

587
00:39:54,281 --> 00:39:57,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I mean, in general, you can find the same in any precept.

588
00:39:57,504 --> 00:39:58,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, same, same.

589
00:39:58,925 --> 00:40:10,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you engage with entertainment, beautification and adornment without implying the ownership of that which you are entertaining, adoring and beautifying?

590
00:40:12,237 --> 00:40:16,823
[SPEAKER_01]: without implying the value of this thing is beautiful and worth embellishment.

591
00:40:17,624 --> 00:40:24,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, would you be embellishing a poisonous drink that just smelling it might kill you, let alone touching it or drinking it?

592
00:40:25,313 --> 00:40:26,254
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't go near it.

593
00:40:26,775 --> 00:40:29,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you then sort of spend your time

594
00:40:30,206 --> 00:40:31,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Like dressing it up.

595
00:40:31,727 --> 00:40:34,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And why do you need to dress it up in the first place?

596
00:40:35,010 --> 00:40:38,973
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if it's already beautiful and wonderful... Would you dress up a pile of excrement?

597
00:40:39,914 --> 00:40:43,397
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of dress will make a pile of excrement attractive.

598
00:40:43,997 --> 00:40:44,518
[SPEAKER_01]: It just wouldn't.

599
00:40:45,058 --> 00:40:57,328
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will only dress up something that you can perceive that can be embellished even more by dressing up, by adorning, by beautifying, by covering up the ugly, so I can emphasize the beautiful, means you imply it's beautiful.

600
00:40:58,636 --> 00:41:01,298
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's already a contradiction in terms, because it isn't beautiful.

601
00:41:01,358 --> 00:41:03,000
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's beautiful, it would be in your control.

602
00:41:03,380 --> 00:41:04,001
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't age.

603
00:41:04,021 --> 00:41:05,382
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be subjected to elements.

604
00:41:05,402 --> 00:41:07,224
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be subjected to dying, sickness.

605
00:41:07,685 --> 00:41:14,031
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be subjected to, basically, everything else that's going on in the body that you conveniently ignore every single time.

606
00:41:14,051 --> 00:41:17,634
[SPEAKER_01]: You choose to only single out one aspect that's not as ugly.

607
00:41:18,535 --> 00:41:19,536
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what we spoke before.

608
00:41:19,836 --> 00:41:22,238
[SPEAKER_01]: People think things are beautiful and then ugly.

609
00:41:22,779 --> 00:41:25,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they can say, oh, this is beautiful and less beautiful.

610
00:41:25,601 --> 00:41:31,125
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, if you practice correctly, sense restraint and develop wisdom, it's gonna be ugly and even more ugly.

611
00:41:31,585 --> 00:41:33,066
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing beautiful anymore.

612
00:41:33,147 --> 00:41:37,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no beauty in danger and peril and deadly suffering and trap.

613
00:41:37,530 --> 00:41:40,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's a perfectly engineered, beautifully dressed trap.

614
00:41:41,153 --> 00:41:43,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still a perilous trap and you are a subject of it.

615
00:41:43,675 --> 00:41:44,835
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will be scared.

616
00:41:45,636 --> 00:41:46,957
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not be beautifying it.

617
00:41:47,017 --> 00:41:49,419
[SPEAKER_01]: You will be highlighting its danger, its ugliness.

618
00:41:50,540 --> 00:41:58,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way you would engage in any form of even that tiny bit of beautification is by not seeing the danger and the ugliness of it, by ignoring it, by choosing to not attend to it.

619
00:41:59,748 --> 00:42:00,929
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe, again, it's a cover-up.

620
00:42:01,429 --> 00:42:02,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a cover-up, again.

621
00:42:03,932 --> 00:42:04,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Another discrepancy.

622
00:42:04,872 --> 00:42:05,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

623
00:42:05,913 --> 00:42:07,715
[SPEAKER_00]: And...

624
00:42:09,516 --> 00:42:13,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in terms of karma action,

625
00:42:16,159 --> 00:42:18,821
[SPEAKER_00]: And the result of action...

626
00:42:21,983 --> 00:42:35,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is... the result of action, the result of your actions, is reality reasserting itself.

627
00:42:38,333 --> 00:42:38,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Which reality?

628
00:42:38,994 --> 00:42:40,875
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you talking about?

629
00:42:40,895 --> 00:42:42,256
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean by reality?

630
00:42:42,576 --> 00:42:43,937
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean the nature of things.

631
00:42:45,677 --> 00:42:49,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

632
00:42:50,022 --> 00:42:52,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's how nature thinks.

633
00:42:52,424 --> 00:42:56,789
[SPEAKER_01]: What reasserts itself is already that attitude of ignoring and covering up.

634
00:42:57,770 --> 00:42:58,771
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the proliferation.

635
00:42:58,811 --> 00:43:03,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you engage, the more of the unwholesome karma you do,

636
00:43:05,167 --> 00:43:12,531
[SPEAKER_01]: The result, the direct result, here and now, will be reinforcing of the underlying ignoring of those values, which is what your value is.

637
00:43:12,551 --> 00:43:19,595
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically underlying wrong views, underlying lack of context of danger, peril, and so on.

638
00:43:19,615 --> 00:43:21,256
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you're reinforcing, that's what grows.

639
00:43:21,336 --> 00:43:25,999
[SPEAKER_00]: It reinforces because it's going against that which is.

640
00:43:28,561 --> 00:43:31,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but that doesn't mean that there which is, it's brought up more.

641
00:43:31,742 --> 00:43:33,303
[SPEAKER_01]: It's simply covered up even more.

642
00:43:33,663 --> 00:43:36,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, but... That's why karma keeps you bound to the trap.

643
00:43:36,905 --> 00:43:37,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

644
00:43:37,385 --> 00:43:44,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that the truth will reveal itself by the more you try to conceal it.

645
00:43:44,248 --> 00:43:54,772
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the more that you try to conceal it, the more you will try to conceal it, because... Yeah, the more bound to concealment of the truth you become, the more dependent on it.

646
00:43:55,032 --> 00:43:56,273
[SPEAKER_00]: It sort of emphasizes...

647
00:43:58,818 --> 00:43:59,018
[SPEAKER_01]: What?

648
00:44:01,381 --> 00:44:04,865
[SPEAKER_01]: The truth that you... No, you wouldn't use the word truth.

649
00:44:05,045 --> 00:44:05,746
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the truth.

650
00:44:05,906 --> 00:44:06,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see it.

651
00:44:06,827 --> 00:44:07,648
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a lie for you.

652
00:44:07,668 --> 00:44:08,809
[SPEAKER_01]: It emphasizes your lie.

653
00:44:09,430 --> 00:44:12,113
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to lie, there has to be an implicit truth.

654
00:44:12,373 --> 00:44:12,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

655
00:44:12,873 --> 00:44:13,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Which you don't know.

656
00:44:13,694 --> 00:44:14,375
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not emphasized.

657
00:44:14,415 --> 00:44:15,777
[SPEAKER_01]: What is emphasized is the lie.

658
00:44:15,957 --> 00:44:16,898
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your starting point.

659
00:44:18,046 --> 00:44:19,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Puthujana has no relationship with the truth.

660
00:44:20,146 --> 00:44:22,387
[SPEAKER_01]: The moment he gets it, he becomes a Sotāpanna.

661
00:44:22,927 --> 00:44:23,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

662
00:44:23,467 --> 00:44:24,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that he knows the truth.

663
00:44:24,848 --> 00:44:25,108
[SPEAKER_01]: I know.

664
00:44:25,228 --> 00:44:28,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why it's not correct to say, oh, reinforces or emphasizes the truth.

665
00:44:28,368 --> 00:44:28,728
[SPEAKER_01]: For whom?

666
00:44:29,669 --> 00:44:31,769
[SPEAKER_01]: For Sotāpanna, that's not the case, because he got the truth.

667
00:44:32,209 --> 00:44:34,890
[SPEAKER_01]: For Puthujana, all thing that's emphasized is a lie.

668
00:44:35,370 --> 00:44:39,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So the statement is abstractly true, what you say, but practically unapplicable.

669
00:44:41,091 --> 00:44:44,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why I... that's why I'm saying it's not correct.

670
00:44:45,042 --> 00:44:52,467
[SPEAKER_01]: I know what you're describing in theory, and yes, but that theory, if it's not applicable to either type of individual, it's not a right theory.

671
00:44:57,471 --> 00:45:01,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, in order to maintain a lie, you kind of carry the truth with you.

672
00:45:04,116 --> 00:45:06,217
[SPEAKER_01]: But that doesn't mean you're a step closer to understanding it.

673
00:45:06,938 --> 00:45:07,198
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

674
00:45:09,720 --> 00:45:13,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So if I do something good, I give... there is a result.

675
00:45:14,904 --> 00:45:20,089
[SPEAKER_00]: That emphasizes that which I've just done.

676
00:45:21,570 --> 00:45:21,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

677
00:45:23,792 --> 00:45:24,412
[SPEAKER_00]: That's...

678
00:45:24,793 --> 00:45:25,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean...

679
00:45:25,173 --> 00:45:25,694
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't...

680
00:45:25,714 --> 00:45:26,915
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I might feel joy...

681
00:45:26,935 --> 00:45:27,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It's non-specific.

682
00:45:27,916 --> 00:45:33,180
[SPEAKER_00]: I might feel joy, I might... you might say thank you, because that act that I've done is good.

683
00:45:34,061 --> 00:45:35,743
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... there's a good result.

684
00:45:35,883 --> 00:45:39,406
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of... there's non... By the way, why is generosity good?

685
00:45:41,570 --> 00:45:42,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it wholesome?

686
00:45:43,272 --> 00:45:46,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is generosity free from discrepancy?

687
00:45:46,776 --> 00:45:52,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still an action, it's still a kamma, but in itself, why is generosity good?

688
00:45:52,922 --> 00:46:01,272
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no concealment there, there's no cover-up, there's no taking away, which is not... No, I'm still very conceited and arrogant and I gave it to you for selfish reasons.

689
00:46:02,327 --> 00:46:03,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But the act of giving is still good.

690
00:46:04,808 --> 00:46:05,868
[SPEAKER_01]: My reason is really selfish.

691
00:46:06,229 --> 00:46:07,029
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't even like you.

692
00:46:07,789 --> 00:46:10,110
[SPEAKER_01]: But yet, that act of giving is still good.

693
00:46:10,230 --> 00:46:15,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because that's the degree of this... nothing's been concealed there.

694
00:46:15,212 --> 00:46:15,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Where?

695
00:46:16,532 --> 00:46:17,173
[SPEAKER_00]: In that whole act.

696
00:46:18,653 --> 00:46:20,974
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it for myself, yes.

697
00:46:20,994 --> 00:46:22,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm doing it for you, yes.

698
00:46:22,755 --> 00:46:24,155
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not pretending that... Well, no.

699
00:46:24,175 --> 00:46:28,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Doing things for yourself is concealment through your own conceit, which already misperceives.

700
00:46:29,805 --> 00:46:32,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I mean... No, no, I'm just talking about the action of giving.

701
00:46:33,468 --> 00:46:35,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the action of taking from you is bad in itself.

702
00:46:36,731 --> 00:46:38,673
[SPEAKER_01]: The action of giving is good in itself.

703
00:46:38,833 --> 00:46:40,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is action of giving good in itself?

704
00:46:41,035 --> 00:46:48,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, I mean, before you were saying that, because it emulates that right order of things.

705
00:46:48,202 --> 00:46:49,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

706
00:46:49,063 --> 00:46:50,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, pretty much, pretty much.

707
00:46:50,524 --> 00:46:53,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I would say differently, in a sense that, like,

708
00:46:54,988 --> 00:47:08,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Giving or taking away, as we... well, as we described in the relation to taking things away, fundamentally, you are in no position to exercise any ownership, even on account of your own sense organs, let alone everything else you can experience through it.

709
00:47:09,831 --> 00:47:12,371
[SPEAKER_01]: So, giving up means relinquishing of ownership.

710
00:47:12,511 --> 00:47:15,192
[SPEAKER_01]: However symbolic, by the way, you don't even need to think about it.

711
00:47:15,772 --> 00:47:19,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Giving something, it mirrors the non-ownership.

712
00:47:20,293 --> 00:47:20,993
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's good.

713
00:47:24,641 --> 00:47:26,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Mirrors the impossibility to own.

714
00:47:26,723 --> 00:47:27,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

715
00:47:28,285 --> 00:47:32,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Mirrors the possibility of relinquishing the ownership, even if you don't make the effort.

716
00:47:32,570 --> 00:47:33,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

717
00:47:33,831 --> 00:47:35,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Good karma will not free you from karma.

718
00:47:36,668 --> 00:47:41,350
[SPEAKER_01]: You can give all you want, but underneath all of that, you still value the ownership.

719
00:47:41,370 --> 00:47:44,592
[SPEAKER_01]: You still imply the ownership of your sense of self, your body, and everything else.

720
00:47:44,852 --> 00:47:47,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But because it emulates that...

721
00:47:48,173 --> 00:47:50,194
[SPEAKER_01]: It emulates that which you don't see nor understand.

722
00:47:50,214 --> 00:47:50,734
[SPEAKER_00]: The right order.

723
00:47:50,774 --> 00:47:55,997
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's... that's why... That's why it's good, and that's why it will make you, like, as Guru would say, you'll be reborn in better realms.

724
00:47:56,457 --> 00:47:56,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

725
00:47:57,017 --> 00:48:03,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, that the reserve for those who have not been so dependent in this life on desire, aversion, and distraction.

726
00:48:03,980 --> 00:48:05,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's more in line with the truth.

727
00:48:06,581 --> 00:48:08,082
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more in line with the nature of things.

728
00:48:08,142 --> 00:48:08,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, you can say...

729
00:48:08,762 --> 00:48:10,422
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more in line with the universal principles.

730
00:48:10,782 --> 00:48:22,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, if I act opposed to those universal principles, I do something unwholesome, I don't know how to say it, is... the result of that

731
00:48:30,027 --> 00:48:30,767
[SPEAKER_01]: The result of that?

732
00:48:30,967 --> 00:48:39,429
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the result of that emphasizes that something is not right.

733
00:48:39,609 --> 00:48:39,989
[SPEAKER_00]: For whom?

734
00:48:41,350 --> 00:48:42,890
[SPEAKER_01]: For a third-person observer?

735
00:48:43,230 --> 00:48:43,550
[SPEAKER_00]: For me.

736
00:48:44,090 --> 00:48:46,171
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, you won't see that.

737
00:48:46,491 --> 00:48:48,431
[SPEAKER_01]: More sensuality will bring more sensuality.

738
00:48:49,851 --> 00:48:52,332
[SPEAKER_01]: More sensuality cannot emphasize the problem of sensuality.

739
00:48:53,708 --> 00:48:59,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Only you turning away from sensuality, like with your own reflection upon the sensuality you engaged.

740
00:49:03,861 --> 00:49:04,341
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

741
00:49:04,901 --> 00:49:07,182
[SPEAKER_01]: You're thinking about it accurately, but abstractly.

742
00:49:07,482 --> 00:49:08,542
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I'm looking at...

743
00:49:08,562 --> 00:49:12,943
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if I'm looking at another person, I'm looking at you acting badly.

744
00:49:13,164 --> 00:49:13,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

745
00:49:13,844 --> 00:49:15,584
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I see you get bad results.

746
00:49:15,844 --> 00:49:18,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I can say I know why you're getting bad results.

747
00:49:19,525 --> 00:49:19,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

748
00:49:19,645 --> 00:49:23,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not, you know, living in accordance with the dhamma.

749
00:49:24,787 --> 00:49:26,487
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going against the nature of things.

750
00:49:26,507 --> 00:49:27,688
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why.

751
00:49:27,748 --> 00:49:28,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but that...

752
00:49:29,933 --> 00:49:36,756
[SPEAKER_01]: But that means you understand the law of kamma and how I got the bad results on account of this, or...?

753
00:49:36,796 --> 00:49:43,778
[SPEAKER_00]: But can't you understand the law of kamma on that basic... No, you can understand the wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome.

754
00:49:43,818 --> 00:49:49,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can know whatever goes through the gateway of contradicting that, maintaining that existential contradiction, will be unwholesome.

755
00:49:49,080 --> 00:49:52,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever goes through the gateway of... And will result in unwholesome.

756
00:49:52,462 --> 00:49:53,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, of course.

757
00:49:53,182 --> 00:49:53,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

758
00:49:53,482 --> 00:49:54,382
[SPEAKER_01]: But to what extent?

759
00:49:54,402 --> 00:49:54,902
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know that.

760
00:49:54,942 --> 00:49:55,323
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, yeah.

761
00:49:55,343 --> 00:49:56,563
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not saying... You don't know that.

762
00:49:56,583 --> 00:49:57,443
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly... Yeah.

763
00:49:59,136 --> 00:50:01,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion and distraction will always result in unwholesome.

764
00:50:03,357 --> 00:50:08,659
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot, as the Buddha said, wholesome action cannot have unwholesome results.

765
00:50:09,239 --> 00:50:11,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Unwholesome action cannot have wholesome results.

766
00:50:11,280 --> 00:50:11,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

767
00:50:14,721 --> 00:50:16,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are you trying to say, still?

768
00:50:26,143 --> 00:50:34,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the universal law, the nature's law, the principles we were talking about, they're either abstractly true or true in case of an arahant.

769
00:50:34,527 --> 00:50:49,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what I was saying was that the result of action is the universal principle asserting itself.

770
00:50:50,334 --> 00:50:51,175
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how I started.

771
00:50:51,335 --> 00:50:51,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

772
00:50:52,615 --> 00:50:53,276
[SPEAKER_00]: Reasserting.

773
00:50:53,596 --> 00:50:56,017
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the right order.

774
00:50:57,551 --> 00:50:58,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, no, that's not right.

775
00:50:58,792 --> 00:50:59,532
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it not right?

776
00:51:00,052 --> 00:51:00,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, explain it.

777
00:51:01,773 --> 00:51:03,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no right order.

778
00:51:03,874 --> 00:51:06,655
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the wrong order, unless you're a Sotāpanna or higher.

779
00:51:08,055 --> 00:51:11,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing you're reasserting is the wrong order of the universal principle.

780
00:51:13,380 --> 00:51:16,323
[SPEAKER_00]: But because there are results of actions.

781
00:51:16,864 --> 00:51:17,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

782
00:51:17,405 --> 00:51:21,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if there was the right order, would there be results of your actions?

783
00:51:21,810 --> 00:51:22,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

784
00:51:23,091 --> 00:51:23,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Your actions.

785
00:51:23,892 --> 00:51:24,172
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

786
00:51:24,192 --> 00:51:27,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you're talking about the order of actual physical action.

787
00:51:27,316 --> 00:51:27,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

788
00:51:28,077 --> 00:51:31,060
[SPEAKER_00]: But except for an arahant, who's gone beyond kamma.

789
00:51:31,220 --> 00:51:31,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

790
00:51:32,682 --> 00:51:33,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he is uprooted in kamma.

791
00:51:34,774 --> 00:51:36,655
[SPEAKER_01]: He hasn't gone beyond it for as long as he's alive.

792
00:51:37,596 --> 00:51:44,021
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, things that he's to experience on the level of body and elements, he will still experience in this life.

793
00:51:44,621 --> 00:51:47,784
[SPEAKER_01]: But there won't be any future lives for him to experience that.

794
00:51:49,965 --> 00:51:58,131
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's what I mean, like, by saying that you're in line with universal principle, you're obscuring the fact that you can do good and still not be free from kamma.

795
00:51:59,752 --> 00:52:01,413
[SPEAKER_01]: You can do bad, you can do good.

796
00:52:01,654 --> 00:52:02,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Both are doing.

797
00:52:03,580 --> 00:52:04,921
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not rooted in the action.

798
00:52:06,162 --> 00:52:10,884
[SPEAKER_01]: They're rooted in whether you see the Universal Principle or you don't see the Universal Principle.

799
00:52:13,966 --> 00:52:21,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Sotāpanna in highest sees the Universal Principle, which is why he has a limited amount of rebirth and eventually he's completely in parinibbāna.

800
00:52:22,732 --> 00:52:28,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Not seeing the Universal Principle means you're still going to be equally reborn just in better places, maybe if you were doing good.

801
00:52:33,212 --> 00:52:39,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, acting out of non-seeing of universal principle, reasserts the not-seeing of universal principle.

802
00:52:40,594 --> 00:52:55,198
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to stop acting out of not-seeing the universal principle, as in, you need to stop doing unwholesome things, you need to start being virtuous and keeping the precepts, and then you might get to see the universal principle, because at least you're not contradicting it anymore, on all layers of action.

803
00:52:56,778 --> 00:52:58,319
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a guarantee you'll still see it.

804
00:52:58,759 --> 00:53:01,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be virtuous for the rest of your life and still remain a bhutujjana.

805
00:53:03,475 --> 00:53:09,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing that matters, whether universal principle makes a difference or not, is whether it's seen or not.

806
00:53:10,117 --> 00:53:12,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Not whether you act with it accordingly or not.

807
00:53:13,558 --> 00:53:16,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You can act with it accordingly and still not understand it.

808
00:53:17,379 --> 00:53:18,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's what I'm saying.

809
00:53:18,519 --> 00:53:20,360
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's wrong what you said.

810
00:53:21,760 --> 00:53:23,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing or not seeing the universal principle is what matters.

811
00:53:25,933 --> 00:53:32,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only time a universal principle will be reasserted, is for the one who saw that, which can be now reasserted.

812
00:53:32,375 --> 00:53:33,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, okay, okay, yeah, okay.

813
00:53:34,455 --> 00:53:38,996
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if... so, you don't see the universal principle, it doesn't mean that it's not there.

814
00:53:40,437 --> 00:53:42,397
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's a possibility of seeing it is there.

815
00:53:43,878 --> 00:53:45,858
[SPEAKER_00]: But, the universal principle is there.

816
00:53:45,878 --> 00:53:46,938
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a universal principle, yeah.

817
00:53:46,958 --> 00:53:47,399
[SPEAKER_00]: You just don't see it.

818
00:53:47,539 --> 00:53:49,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, it wouldn't be a universal principle.

819
00:53:49,519 --> 00:53:53,000
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't see it, it doesn't mean that it's not... it's not the case.

820
00:53:54,742 --> 00:53:56,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't... What universal principle?

821
00:53:56,483 --> 00:53:58,304
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no universal principle, because I don't see it.

822
00:53:58,584 --> 00:54:01,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're basically perverting it.

823
00:54:01,946 --> 00:54:03,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're bound by it wrongly.

824
00:54:04,347 --> 00:54:11,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but that's why then I would be affected by actions.

825
00:54:13,332 --> 00:54:15,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's correct.

826
00:54:15,673 --> 00:54:19,555
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not correct to say that that's why you would be reaffirming the universal principle.

827
00:54:25,700 --> 00:54:26,061
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

828
00:54:26,922 --> 00:54:28,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't mean to actually imply that.

829
00:54:28,544 --> 00:54:29,425
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't mean to imply that.

830
00:54:29,465 --> 00:54:30,386
[SPEAKER_00]: I know, I know, I know.

831
00:54:30,406 --> 00:54:31,568
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's how you were phrasing it.

832
00:54:31,928 --> 00:54:32,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

833
00:54:32,889 --> 00:54:35,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you reassert the universal principle by acting out of it.

834
00:54:36,554 --> 00:54:36,694
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

835
00:54:40,146 --> 00:54:45,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out cannot reassert the universal principle, because acting out is already within the universal principle.

836
00:54:46,370 --> 00:55:00,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that universal principle, that your action cannot step outside, or affirm or deny, your action is the universal principle, can either be seen, which will then free you from action, or not seen, which keeps you bound with the action, and the direction that that action takes.

837
00:55:01,119 --> 00:55:02,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeps you bound with the action.

838
00:55:02,120 --> 00:55:02,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

839
00:55:02,860 --> 00:55:05,942
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I think, if you want to understand why your statement was wrong to begin with,

840
00:55:06,936 --> 00:55:10,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop implying that your action is kind of a response to universal principle.

841
00:55:11,217 --> 00:55:13,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Action is fully enclosed within the universal principle.

842
00:55:14,018 --> 00:55:15,119
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot step outside of it.

843
00:55:15,479 --> 00:55:16,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

844
00:55:16,219 --> 00:55:20,821
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, I'm... Good action, bad action, neutral action, within the universal principle.

845
00:55:21,161 --> 00:55:26,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wouldn't say that that which is already within the universal principle is reaffirming the universal principle by acting.

846
00:55:26,543 --> 00:55:27,183
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

847
00:55:27,263 --> 00:55:30,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Reaffirmation of your action is, again, within the same universal principle.

848
00:55:31,469 --> 00:55:34,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether that principle is understood or not, that's where the issue is.

849
00:55:35,216 --> 00:55:41,046
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Sotāpanna is one who knows good kamma as good kamma and bad kamma as bad kamma.

850
00:55:41,066 --> 00:55:42,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, he doesn't know the results of it.

851
00:55:42,829 --> 00:55:43,851
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows the nature of it.

852
00:55:44,547 --> 00:55:58,052
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he understands that this action is within the right direction of the universal principle, and that he understands that this direction is within the wrong... sorry, this action is within the wrong direction within the universal principle, which is why it's wrong.

853
00:55:58,112 --> 00:56:03,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But the pratyujana acts, but doesn't know the universal principle, which is... That's why he's not free from karma.

854
00:56:03,713 --> 00:56:05,074
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he doesn't know that he's acting...

855
00:56:06,316 --> 00:56:07,077
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't know.

856
00:56:07,477 --> 00:56:16,106
[SPEAKER_01]: He can take on faith, on trust, on authority, the reasons for acting good, but that doesn't mean he understood the nature of action.

857
00:56:16,387 --> 00:56:18,629
[SPEAKER_00]: So he... but he's affected by his actions.

858
00:56:20,131 --> 00:56:23,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, his actions will take him in the direction that basically you choose them to.

859
00:56:23,855 --> 00:56:25,556
[SPEAKER_00]: But if he knows the universal principle?

860
00:56:25,977 --> 00:56:28,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Then that... how far you will go in that direction will be limited.

861
00:56:30,308 --> 00:56:36,891
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't be affected or not... At most in this life, if he's an arahant, and then he'll... he will not be affected.

862
00:56:37,092 --> 00:56:38,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Like Angulimala.

863
00:56:39,113 --> 00:56:51,879
[SPEAKER_01]: When he went to Pindapad, later on, when he became an arahant, and some people recognized him, and they threw stones and sticks at him, and beating him, and he came back bloodied, bruised, like in pain, and the Buddha said, ah, bear it, Brahmin, bear it.

864
00:56:52,219 --> 00:56:57,842
[SPEAKER_01]: You are bearing out results of your actions now, which you would have done otherwise for eons to come.

865
00:56:59,047 --> 00:56:59,667
[SPEAKER_01]: But this is it.

866
00:56:59,727 --> 00:57:04,528
[SPEAKER_01]: This is all you're gonna have left over from the horrible things you've done is these few stones.

867
00:57:04,788 --> 00:57:07,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they are... those are the results of his previous actions.

868
00:57:07,589 --> 00:57:18,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Although he's an arahant, you realize these results are on the level of elements, other people, everything else, basically, that can befall this body, these five aggregates.

869
00:57:18,792 --> 00:57:23,113
[SPEAKER_01]: But he has a limit because he's out by being an arahant.

870
00:57:23,333 --> 00:57:27,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when this breaks apart, these aggregates, they will be not new ones being taken up.

871
00:57:28,646 --> 00:57:36,872
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the amount of that direction that can be suffered, endured or enjoyed on account of your good previous actions is sort of cut off, finished.

872
00:57:37,212 --> 00:57:40,574
[SPEAKER_01]: For Sotāpanna, as we say, seven lifetimes, anāgāmī once returned.

873
00:57:40,594 --> 00:57:47,979
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was a measure of your progress and how far you can sort of carry on as a result of previous actions, but that's it.

874
00:57:51,481 --> 00:57:53,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because for the Sotāpanna, for whatever he does,

875
00:57:56,097 --> 00:58:00,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be within that span, at most, or even less.

876
00:58:00,581 --> 00:58:04,024
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever he does... Let me try again that same thing.

877
00:58:04,245 --> 00:58:07,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever he does, reasserts the universal principle.

878
00:58:09,017 --> 00:58:11,098
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, it reminds him of that universal principle.

879
00:58:11,298 --> 00:58:12,658
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because he has understood it.

880
00:58:12,818 --> 00:58:13,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever...

881
00:58:13,378 --> 00:58:21,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say... That's what I mean, like, if you speak in a third-person sense, you can say universal principle is there.

882
00:58:21,281 --> 00:58:23,301
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I said, element of nibbāna is present.

883
00:58:23,462 --> 00:58:26,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You can... Everybody can, theoretically, develop nibbāna.

884
00:58:27,343 --> 00:58:33,885
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that the one, like, from the point of view of a person, if you're not seeing it, you're not having it.

885
00:58:34,025 --> 00:58:34,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

886
00:58:34,905 --> 00:58:37,706
[SPEAKER_01]: But a Sotāpanna has... Sotāpanna seeing it, he has it.

887
00:58:37,846 --> 00:58:40,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yes, everything would be reaffirming.

888
00:58:40,808 --> 00:58:44,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if he does unwholesome, he will be reminded of the wholesome underlying framework.

889
00:58:44,869 --> 00:58:45,930
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why he can't go that far.

890
00:58:46,070 --> 00:58:48,171
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he can't go that far in unwholesome.

891
00:58:48,491 --> 00:58:51,752
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't go that far in all the actions you can do on account of breaking the precepts.

892
00:58:51,792 --> 00:58:56,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why he can't go that far in on account of the rebirths that will wait for you on account of those bad actions.

893
00:58:56,394 --> 00:58:57,255
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't go that far in

894
00:58:57,695 --> 00:59:06,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, like, we can say the statement you said, everything you do reaffirms or reasserts the universal principle is true for Sotāpanna or higher, not for the one below.

895
00:59:06,839 --> 00:59:16,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you allow puthujjana to partake in that statement, that would equal, like, when people read, oh, the element of nibbāna is present in everyone's experience,

896
00:59:17,043 --> 00:59:22,870
[SPEAKER_01]: And then people assume, ah, we're already in nibbāna, as you have the Mahāyāna schools, saying the Buddha nature and nibbāna is always there.

897
00:59:22,930 --> 00:59:23,771
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to do anything.

898
00:59:24,452 --> 00:59:28,997
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like greed, aversion, delusion are symptoms of non-Buddha nature and non-nibbāna.

899
00:59:30,058 --> 00:59:35,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from greed, aversion, delusion means, yes, you can get your Buddha nature and your nibbāna, but you can't have it both.

900
00:59:36,766 --> 00:59:37,466
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which one is it?

901
00:59:38,415 --> 00:59:49,041
[SPEAKER_01]: But if people develop a view that this abstract possibility of nibbāna is what nibbāna is, they will not get free from the symptoms that prevent them to get nibbāna here and now.

902
00:59:50,101 --> 00:59:51,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the Buddha talked about it.

903
00:59:51,242 --> 00:59:57,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when a man not free from greed, aversion, delusion, says, oh, I perceive nibbāna, I am in nibbāna, I'm attaining nibbāna.

904
00:59:57,265 --> 01:00:01,988
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yeah, sometimes, if he made the effort, he would be factually stating that which nibbāna is.

905
01:00:02,208 --> 01:00:05,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But he wouldn't be attaining it, because he's not free from his own conceit

906
01:00:08,165 --> 01:00:09,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not the nibbāna.

907
01:00:10,588 --> 01:00:13,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's not the universal principle, because you're not seeing it.

908
01:00:14,552 --> 01:00:21,900
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just abstractly not applicable to your experience that contradicts it through greed, aversion and dullness and distraction.

909
01:00:25,164 --> 01:00:29,849
[SPEAKER_01]: So, theoretically, you are within the universal principle, either, but you're going against it.

910
01:00:31,436 --> 01:00:36,538
[SPEAKER_01]: By not being free from greed, aversion, delusion, means your values are against the universal principle.

911
01:00:37,138 --> 01:00:45,801
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you act out of the values that are against it, the more you're reinforcing the againstness of values, not that which you're against.

912
01:00:48,102 --> 01:00:58,726
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to end or recap, I was saying, what is common to everything that's unwholesome, is that

913
01:01:01,727 --> 01:01:10,976
[SPEAKER_00]: That view of... or that act of misappropriating things, taking that which is not yours, concealing that which is... Yeah.

914
01:01:11,097 --> 01:01:16,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's in common, by the way, to misappropriating, concealing, taking up?

915
01:01:17,823 --> 01:01:18,404
[SPEAKER_01]: The attitude.

916
01:01:18,684 --> 01:01:20,026
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the common thread?

917
01:01:21,287 --> 01:01:22,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Mūḍhapareyāya 1.

918
01:01:28,175 --> 01:01:29,336
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

919
01:01:29,436 --> 01:01:31,057
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of all things.

920
01:01:31,717 --> 01:01:34,739
[SPEAKER_01]: All things are rooted in conceiving.

921
01:01:34,759 --> 01:01:40,183
[SPEAKER_01]: See, the fact that you're conceiving already means misconceiving.

922
01:01:40,924 --> 01:01:47,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Already means putting yourself first, that second, taking the own, implying the ownership, lack of... All of that comes from simple conceiving.

923
01:01:47,768 --> 01:01:50,911
[SPEAKER_01]: I am in this, I am from this, this is for me, and so on.

924
01:01:51,808 --> 01:01:53,070
[SPEAKER_00]: This is for me, therefore I am.

925
01:01:53,210 --> 01:01:55,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, all that, all that.

926
01:01:56,315 --> 01:02:01,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was trying to come up with a term for that.

927
01:02:02,285 --> 01:02:06,392
[SPEAKER_00]: A patujana, in that sense, is one who conceives, is a thief.

928
01:02:08,863 --> 01:02:09,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

929
01:02:10,324 --> 01:02:15,066
[SPEAKER_01]: However, like, that wouldn't be... it's kind of more like a... it would be an impassionate statement.

930
01:02:15,086 --> 01:02:15,566
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a thief.

931
01:02:15,986 --> 01:02:19,488
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not accurate, because in order to be a thief, you need to perceive the lack of ownership.

932
01:02:19,988 --> 01:02:20,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Tujana doesn't perceive that.

933
01:02:21,408 --> 01:02:23,429
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's a victim of his own ignorance.

934
01:02:24,590 --> 01:02:27,031
[SPEAKER_01]: That results in him being... acting like a thief, and so on.

935
01:02:28,431 --> 01:02:34,574
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that's not... that conceiving and ignoring these things... it's not on the level of lying to yourself.

936
01:02:35,545 --> 01:02:37,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order to lie to yourself, you know the truth.

937
01:02:37,847 --> 01:02:38,847
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just trying to cover it up.

938
01:02:38,867 --> 01:02:49,314
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just ignoring and choosing to always emphasize a certain direction of least resistance, of pleasure, avoidance of pain, all the values that a mind of Bhutujana would be acting out of.

939
01:02:49,454 --> 01:02:51,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality, ill-will and delusion.

940
01:02:53,537 --> 01:02:59,061
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can now equally choose to not act to, which will then start to reveal that which you have been ignoring.

941
01:03:01,903 --> 01:03:02,363
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

942
01:03:02,483 --> 01:03:05,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Your attention is already implying ignorance.

943
01:03:06,480 --> 01:03:10,041
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to ignore the context, you want to ignore centrality.

944
01:03:10,541 --> 01:03:17,263
[SPEAKER_01]: By all means, by the way, engage in avijja towards centrality, towards ill-will and towards delusion and distraction.

945
01:03:18,723 --> 01:03:19,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Break the precepts.

946
01:03:19,743 --> 01:03:25,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Kill avijja, kill the passion, kill ill-will, kill the distraction.

947
01:03:26,265 --> 01:03:26,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Get rid of it.

948
01:03:27,765 --> 01:03:28,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Do away with it.

949
01:03:30,826 --> 01:03:31,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I said.

950
01:03:31,706 --> 01:03:33,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, there are things you should kill.

951
01:03:34,057 --> 01:03:35,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

952
01:03:35,538 --> 01:03:43,043
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just keep choosing to ignore other things because you keep choosing to follow pleasure and avoid pain, at face value.

953
01:03:44,604 --> 01:03:49,928
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start making the effort on account of Buddha's instruction, no, don't attend to this, don't attend to that.

954
01:03:50,749 --> 01:03:53,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignore possibility of sensuality.

955
01:03:53,891 --> 01:03:55,172
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you need to do, by the way.

956
01:03:55,552 --> 01:03:59,175
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you ignore everything else when you choose to go down the path of sensuality.

957
01:03:59,935 --> 01:04:02,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ignore sensuality and go everywhere else.

958
01:04:05,391 --> 01:04:07,433
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is essential desire, ignore it.

959
01:04:09,434 --> 01:04:11,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't welcome it, don't entertain it, don't engage with it, don't feed it.

960
01:04:11,996 --> 01:04:12,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ignore it.

961
01:04:13,297 --> 01:04:14,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Let it die away.

962
01:04:17,120 --> 01:04:18,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Reassert the universal principle.

963
01:04:19,562 --> 01:04:23,465
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by ignoring the desire, now you automatically have to look the other way.

964
01:04:24,185 --> 01:04:27,368
[SPEAKER_00]: The way you've been ignoring, by giving in to the desire.

965
01:04:31,362 --> 01:04:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why attention is not, as Buddha said, is not a single thing in the world that will bring sensuality to increase and non-sensuality to decrease as improper attention, as wrong intention.

966
01:04:42,914 --> 01:04:48,761
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is not a single thing in the world that will bring sensuality to decrease and non-sensuality to increase as proper attention.

967
01:04:50,042 --> 01:04:52,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when there is a prospect of sensual desire present itself,

968
01:04:54,970 --> 01:04:58,092
[SPEAKER_01]: You can take it at a face value and start attending to its beautiful aspects.

969
01:04:58,172 --> 01:04:59,593
[SPEAKER_01]: They would be entertaining you even more.

970
01:05:00,994 --> 01:05:11,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, implicitly, you're ignoring the context of peril of it, danger, lack of satisfaction, poison, basically, the threat, the trap, everything in it.

971
01:05:11,260 --> 01:05:11,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-beautiful.

972
01:05:11,700 --> 01:05:12,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-beautiful, yeah.

973
01:05:12,941 --> 01:05:17,384
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you can choose to make the effort to restrain yourself from going down the path of sensuality offered.

974
01:05:18,124 --> 01:05:28,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, start to reflect on the greater picture, and then, automatically, you cannot be aware of the greater picture of sensuality, which is, oh, this is not really that satisfactory, and at the same time, be equally attracted to it.

975
01:05:29,147 --> 01:05:29,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

976
01:05:29,747 --> 01:05:31,107
[SPEAKER_01]: It's mutually exclusive.

977
01:05:31,127 --> 01:05:36,148
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start making effort in the direction of greater picture, you will be less and less pulled by the sensuality.

978
01:05:43,010 --> 01:05:46,791
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your attention determines the direction where your mind will be proliferating to.

979
01:05:49,074 --> 01:05:55,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you attend to centrality, the more you're leaning towards it, the more you will attend to it, the more you lean towards it.

980
01:05:55,304 --> 01:05:58,309
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why some people are more dependent on central distraction, some people less.

981
01:05:59,594 --> 01:05:59,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

982
01:06:00,995 --> 01:06:08,940
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have that bigger picture, there's... You have to start cultivating the bigger picture by not taking the little picture at face value and just jumping down it.

983
01:06:08,960 --> 01:06:09,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

984
01:06:09,480 --> 01:06:15,323
[SPEAKER_01]: So sensory strain needs to come first, virtue needs to come first, and then you get to, oh, there is a bigger picture.

985
01:06:15,964 --> 01:06:26,710
[SPEAKER_01]: But if now I start making even more effort on account of the bigger picture towards the bigger picture and attend that at the expense of attending to the particular picture within it, you'll start withdrawing from centrality.

986
01:06:26,942 --> 01:06:31,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no matter how far you've proliferated... No matter how far you've proliferated, the way is always up.

987
01:06:31,685 --> 01:06:32,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You can go out of it.

988
01:06:32,446 --> 01:06:36,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter how far your preferences went before.

989
01:06:37,369 --> 01:06:41,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that thing can... yeah, it's a dispassion towards that, when you see this.

990
01:06:41,171 --> 01:06:43,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a dispassion towards whole domain, exactly.

991
01:06:43,313 --> 01:06:46,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as you said, that tree, a dead tree.

992
01:06:46,900 --> 01:06:47,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

993
01:06:47,280 --> 01:06:48,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a palm stump.

994
01:06:48,061 --> 01:06:49,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Palm stump cut off at the root.

995
01:06:49,623 --> 01:06:50,383
[SPEAKER_01]: You kill the roots.

996
01:06:50,463 --> 01:06:53,967
[SPEAKER_01]: All these branches of... they proliferated in the past.

997
01:06:54,047 --> 01:06:55,328
[SPEAKER_01]: While you were feeding it, they stayed.

998
01:06:55,628 --> 01:06:56,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's gone.

999
01:06:56,509 --> 01:06:57,430
[SPEAKER_01]: The life of it is gone.

1000
01:06:58,591 --> 01:07:01,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's... that was... that's the whole point.

1001
01:07:01,033 --> 01:07:02,855
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to be now cutting the branches down.

1002
01:07:02,875 --> 01:07:03,215
[SPEAKER_01]: They're dead.

1003
01:07:03,395 --> 01:07:04,016
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not going to grow.

1004
01:07:04,736 --> 01:07:06,118
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot induce more sensuality.

1005
01:07:06,138 --> 01:07:07,599
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all finished.

1006
01:07:07,759 --> 01:07:10,562
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why an arahant would still remember things he used to enjoy.

1007
01:07:11,543 --> 01:07:11,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1008
01:07:12,125 --> 01:07:13,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I used to like this, I used to not like that.

1009
01:07:14,406 --> 01:07:18,688
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to have wives, do like whatever, detain me with the music at night and so on.

1010
01:07:19,388 --> 01:07:23,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I have no desire towards that anymore, but I still remember what I used to enjoy while I was ignorant.

